\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces TempoRAC\xspace  . A frozen video transformer reads the video once at frame strides 2, 4 and 8 and at two depths; overlapping clips are averaged into token streams on a shared frame grid, pooled over space, fused per cell with a learned dataset token, processed by a temporal encoder and decoded into an event density whose sum is the count. An additional stride-16 encoding supports a shifted view, with exact input correspondence on unpadded clips and source-endpoint padding (\cref  {sec:shift}). It is used as training augmentation and as a second decoder pass at test time; the reported count averages the two views. One decoder is trained on the union of RepCount-A, UCFRep and Countix.}}{2}{figure.caption.1}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:framework}{{1}{2}{\method . A frozen video transformer reads the video once at frame strides 2, 4 and 8 and at two depths; overlapping clips are averaged into token streams on a shared frame grid, pooled over space, fused per cell with a learned dataset token, processed by a temporal encoder and decoded into an event density whose sum is the count. An additional stride-16 encoding supports a shifted view, with exact input correspondence on unpadded clips and source-endpoint padding (\cref {sec:shift}). It is used as training augmentation and as a second decoder pass at test time; the reported count averages the two views. One decoder is trained on the union of RepCount-A, UCFRep and Countix}{figure.caption.1}{}}
\newlabel{fig:framework@cref}{{[figure][1][]1}{[1][1][]2}{}{}{}}
